\subsection{Four-Seed Replication}
\label{sec:replication}

The favorable-region result rests on Mistral-7B FOLIO performance, so we replicate with four random seeds to assess stability. All four runs use identical hyperparameters ($K = 12$, $T = 0.7$, $T_{\text{ext}} = 0.3$) on the full FOLIO evaluation set ($n = 204$).

Seed~42 produces $\Delta = +3.43$\,pp ($p = 0.092$, SC $= 55.88\%$). Seed~123 shows a consistent direction at $\Delta = +2.94$\,pp ($p = 0.070$, SC $= 54.41\%$). Seed~456 shows a null result ($\Delta = 0.00$\,pp, $p = 1.0$, SC $= 55.88\%$). Seed~789 shows a negative result ($\Delta = -0.49$\,pp, $p = 1.0$, SC $= 54.41\%$). No seed reaches statistical significance at $\alpha = 0.05$; 2 of 4 show marginal positive trends ($p < 0.10$). The mean effect across four seeds is $\Delta = +1.47$\,pp, with effects ranging from $+3.43$ to $-0.49$\,pp. Cross-seed variance is high, confirming that even the most favorable condition produces unreliable improvements.

The variance is informative. Seeds~42 and~456 share identical SC baselines (55.88\%) yet produce different SICA outcomes ($+3.43$ vs.\ $0.00$\,pp), indicating that SC level alone does not determine the effect; seed-level constraint quality also matters. Sampling noise at the regime boundary can push individual seeds across the transition, which makes the effect fragile by design rather than by accident. This seed-level sensitivity motivates the entropy decomposition in \S\ref{sec:entropy}, where per-problem trace agreement reveals that the aggregate $\Delta$ is driven entirely by the high-entropy subpopulation: seed~42 achieves $+12.8$\,pp on high-entropy problems while seed~456 shows $-1.3$\,pp on the same partition.

